### How do you determine the owning side?

The foreign key is one column on one table. The orders table has a user_id column, and there is no column on the users table pointing at orders. So the Order entity is the owning side and carries the foreign-key mapping.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "order_id")
    private String orderId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)     // override default EAGER
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id")          // the actual FK column
    private User user;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Column(name = "status")
    private String status;

    // getters, setters
    public void setUser(User user) { this.user = user; }
    public User getUser() { return user; }
}
```

```text
Order is the OWNING side:
- orders table has user_id column (the FK)
- @JoinColumn(name="user_id") names the FK column
- Hibernate reads this field to generate INSERT/UPDATE with user_id
```

### How do you map the inverse side?

The User entity carries @OneToMany(mappedBy="user"). The value of mappedBy is the name of the Java FIELD on Order, not a column name. It tells Hibernate: I am not the owner; read the FK from the order's user field.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "user_id")
    private String userId;

    @Column(name = "name", nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();

    // Sync helpers
    public void addOrder(Order order) {
        orders.add(order);          // inverse side (navigation)
        order.setUser(this);        // OWNING side (drives SQL)
    }

    public void removeOrder(Order order) {
        orders.remove(order);
        order.setUser(null);        // orphanRemoval -> DELETE
    }
}
```

### Why are cascade and orphanRemoval important?

cascade=CascadeType.ALL means that persisting the user also persists new orders, merging the user also merges orders, and removing the user also removes all orders. orphanRemoval=true means that removing an order from the collection deletes it, even while the user continues to exist.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import com.bank.repository.UserRepository;
import com.bank.model.User;
import com.bank.model.Order;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public OrderService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void placeOrder(String userId, String orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        User user = userRepository.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
        Order order = new Order();
        order.setOrderId(orderId);
        order.setAmount(amount);
        order.setStatus("PENDING");
        user.addOrder(order);    // sets both sides
        // cascade=ALL: saving user cascades INSERT for the new order
        userRepository.save(user);
    }

    @Transactional
    public void cancelOrder(String userId, String orderId) {
        User user = userRepository.findWithOrders(userId);
        user.getOrders().removeIf(o -> o.getOrderId().equals(orderId));
        // orphanRemoval=true: removed order is DELETEd on flush
    }
}
```

```text
placeOrder:
  INSERT INTO orders (order_id, user_id, amount, status) VALUES ('ORD001', 'USR001', 50000.00, 'PENDING')
  (cascaded from user.save())

cancelOrder:
  DELETE FROM orders WHERE order_id = 'ORD001'
  (triggered by orphanRemoval when order removed from collection)
```

### Why must both sides be kept in sync?

Because only the owning side drives the foreign-key SQL, adding an order only to the user's collection leaves user_id null. The sync helpers prevent this.

```java
// WRONG: only updating inverse side
user.getOrders().add(order);   // order.user is still null
// INSERT INTO orders (order_id, user_id, amount) VALUES ('ORD001', NULL, 50000)

// CORRECT: sync helper updates both
user.addOrder(order);          // sets order.user = this
// INSERT INTO orders (order_id, user_id, amount) VALUES ('ORD001', 'USR001', 50000)
```

### How do you avoid N+1 with JOIN FETCH?

Never rely on eager loading. Use a JOIN FETCH query to load the user with all orders in a single query. Return DTOs from the service to prevent lazy associations from leaking to the JSON serializer.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import com.bank.model.User;

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, String> {

    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u LEFT JOIN FETCH u.orders WHERE u.userId = :id")
    User findWithOrders(String id);
}
```

```text
Single query:
SELECT u.*, o.* FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.user_id = o.user_id
WHERE u.user_id = 'USR001'

Without JOIN FETCH (N+1):
SELECT u.* FROM users u WHERE u.user_id = 'USR001'     -- 1 query
SELECT o.* FROM orders o WHERE o.user_id = 'USR001'     -- N queries per user
```

### What is the complete entity setup?

Here is the full end-to-end mapping with all decisions applied: owning side, fetch type, cascade, orphan removal, and sync helpers.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "order_id")
    private String orderId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", nullable = false)
    private User user;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Column(name = "status")
    private String status;

    // constructors, getters, setters
    public Order() {}
    public void setOrderId(String orderId) { this.orderId = orderId; }
    public String getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public void setUser(User user) { this.user = user; }
    public void setAmount(BigDecimal amount) { this.amount = amount; }
    public void setStatus(String status) { this.status = status; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "user_id")
    private String userId;

    @Column(name = "name", nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addOrder(Order order) {
        orders.add(order);
        order.setUser(this);
    }

    public void removeOrder(Order order) {
        orders.remove(order);
        order.setUser(null);
    }

    // constructors, getters
    public List<Order> getOrders() { return orders; }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is adding a child only to the collection without setting the owning reference. A second mistake is using CascadeType.ALL on shared reference data, which could delete data another parent still needs.

```java
// WRONG: cascade ALL on shared reference data
@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
private List<Currency> currencies;  // shared! Deleting one user deletes currencies for ALL!

// CORRECT: cascade only to owned children
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
private List<Order> orders;  // orders belong to this user only
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer returns DTOs from the service rather than entities so lazy associations never leak to the JSON serializer. They base Set collection equality on a stable business key. They keep the transaction boundary on the service method. They apply cascade and orphan removal only to genuinely owned children.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public record UserWithOrders(
    String userId,
    String name,
    List<OrderDto> orders
) {}

public record OrderDto(
    String orderId,
    BigDecimal amount,
    String status
) {}
```
